      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224417",
      ":question/problem": "An email server manager determined that $4%$ of the emails their clients receive are spam. After introducing a new filtering feature, the manager wants to know if the proportion of spam emails their clients receive has decreased. They decide to conduct a hypothesis test at the $10%$ significance level.\n\nThe manager samples $100$ client emails and notices that only $1$ is spam. The manager compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the following cumulative distribution table for $X\u223cB(100, 0.04)$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.0169$ | $0.0872$ | $0.2321$ | $0.4295$ | $0.6289$ |\n\nFill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of spam emails in a random sample of $100$, then a suitable test statistic is\n$X = 1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nSince $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $10%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.\n\nTherefore, we {{field-6}} that the proportion of spam emails has reduced since the filtering feature was installed."
    },
