      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224441",
      ":question/problem": "In a factory, it is determined that $3%$ of the items produced by one of the machines are defective. After making improvements and innovations to the machine, an engineer wants to determine if the proportion of defective items that the machine generates has decreased. They decide to conduct a hypothesis test at the $5%$ significance level.\n\nThe engineer samples $200$ items produced by the machine after the improvement and notices that only $2$ of them are defective. The manager compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the following cumulative distribution table for $X\u223cB(200, 0.03)$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.0023$ | $0.0162$ | $0.0593$ | $0.1472$ | $0.2810$ |\n\nFill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of defective items in a random sample of $200$, then a suitable test statistic is\n$X = 2$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nSince $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $5%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.\n\nTherefore, we {{field-6}} that the proportion of defective items after the improvements has reduced."
